From: Sirius port
Subject: [PATCH 12/13] ARM: dts: qcom: msm8974: Krait CPU clocks, supply and OPPs

Nothing in the tree instantiates the Krait clock controller: none of the ARM
qcom device trees in 6.16.12 has a krait-cc node, a CPU OPP table or a
cpu-supply, so every MSM8974 runs at whatever rate its bootloader left.
On a Sony Xperia Z2 that is 960 MHz against a rated 2265.6 MHz.

Add the five HFPLLs, the Krait clock controller and the speed/PVS fuse cell,
and give each CPU its clock, the shared supply and the OPP table.

  - HFPLL and fuse addresses from Sony's device tree
    (qcom,clock-krait@f9016000). The fuse cell sits at 0xb0 in the corrected
    qfprom region; on a Z2 it decodes to speed bin 1, PVS 13, v1 with both
    blow-status bits set, and bin 1's 2265.6 MHz maximum is the part's
    rating.
  - krait-cc-v2 takes no aux clock from the device tree: it derives acpu_aux
    from gpll0_vote itself. It sits at the root, not under soc: it has no
    registers of its own, so it cannot be a simple-bus child.
  - The supply is the L2 SAW, which drives the ganged PM8841 phases for all
    four cores (see the spm patch). It is marked always-on.
  - The OPP table is generated from Sony's factory tables
    (qcom,speed<S>-pvs<P>-bin-v<V>) into qcom-msm8974-krait-opp.dtsi, using
    the opp-microvolt-speed<S>-pvs<P>-v<V> names qcom-cpufreq-nvmem selects
    at runtime. Each entry is a <target target max> triplet: four CPUs share
    one regulator with independent policies, and exact single values from
    different cores would have no voltage in common.
  - Not opp-shared: each Krait core has its own HFPLL and primary mux.

This first version enables operating points up to 960 MHz only, the rate the
cores already sustain, so the supply path can be verified before anything
runs faster. Raising the cap means regenerating the table with
cpufreq/gen-krait-opp.py --cap-hz.

Depends on patches 10, 11 and 13.

